auth:
  symbol: "#auth-route"
  description: >
    Public account endpoints backing the storefront. POST /auth/signup creates a
    user (409 email_taken on conflict), POST /auth/login verifies credentials; both
    return { token, user } where token is a signed JWT from #auth-service. Login
    returns a generic 401 invalid_credentials without revealing which field failed.
    No gate — these are the entry points that mint the ^authenticated token.
  signals: [!user-registered, !user-logged-in]

products:
  symbol: "#products-route"
  description: >
    Public catalog read (GET /products) for the storefront. No auth — browsing is
    anonymous; ^authenticated only applies at /checkout. Prices returned as strings
    (Postgres numeric) so the client never does float math on money.

checkout:
  symbol: "#checkout-route"
  description: >
    The HUMAN (in-browser) leg of $checkout-flow. POST /checkout requires a user-audience
    JWT (^authenticated); an agent grant is rejected here so it can't bypass the selfie
    path. Recomputes price server-side, enforces a 428 confirmation_required above the
    $10 auto-approve threshold — UNLESS the user has an enrolled Ledger device
    (#ledger-confirm-service), in which case ^ledger-confirmed REPLACES the spoofable
    checkoutConfirmed boolean entirely: once the order is reserved,
    resolveLedgerGate(order) must resolve to "approved" (428 ledger_confirmation_required
    with an approvalId while pending, 403 ledger_confirmation_denied on denial/timeout —
    never falls back to checkoutConfirmed for that user) before charging. Users with no
    enrolled device keep today's checkoutConfirmed behavior unchanged. Reserves the order via
    #orders-service.reserveOrder() — which dedups a resubmission (optional body
    `idempotencyKey`, else a short window on user+sku+quantity+amount) and atomically
    enforces ^checkout-authorized (per-tx/daily caps + the Circle Policy Engine hook)
    before inserting — then charges via #payment-service only when a genuinely new
    order was reserved; a deduped resubmission returns the existing order's state
    without charging again. The executePayment()/markOrderPaid() call is wrapped in a
    try/catch: any exception (Circle API error, network blip, missing token balance)
    marks the order failed via #orders-service.markOrderFailed() and returns 502
    payment_failed, instead of leaving the order stuck 'pending' (which would silently
    eat into the user's daily spend cap and wedge its idempotency key). Shares all
    order/spend primitives with #agent-route through #orders-service. Full design in
    .paradigm/specs/wallet-checkout.md.
  gates: [^authenticated, ^checkout-authorized, ^ledger-confirmed]
  signals: [!payment-submitted]

agent:
  symbol: "#agent-route"
  description: >
    The agentic-commerce entry points, called by a merchant MCP holding an agent grant.
    POST /agent/grant (^authenticated) mints the ^agent-authorized grant AND, via
    #agent-grants-service.recordAgentGrant(), a scoped "session key" spend-policy row
    (destination = merchantPayoutAddress(), per-tx/rolling caps, its own TTL — shorter
    than the grant JWT's own 365d lifetime) tied to the grant by its JWT `jti`. POST
    /agent/grant/:grantId/revoke (^authenticated, owner-scoped) cuts a grant's scope off
    early. POST /agent/checkout (^agent-authorized + ^checkout-authorized) validates and
    reprices, rejects early (403) if the grant's scope is already revoked/expired, then
    reserves the order via #orders-service.reserveOrder() — same dedup + atomic
    spend-cap/Circle-policy check as #checkout-route, now also tagging the order with
    its originating grant's scoped-policy id — and creates a PENDING order + a
    verification session, returning 202 verification_required with a human-facing
    selfie URL; it NEVER charges. A deduped resubmission (agent retry) reuses the
    existing order and its still-usable verification session instead of minting a new
    one. GET /agent/purchase/:orderId (^agent-authorized) polls the outcome, scoped to
    the grant's own user. Implements $agent-purchase-flow; reuses #orders-service and
    #payment-service so both checkout legs stay in lockstep. The grant-scope policy
    itself is an app-level session key, not an on-chain one — see
    .paradigm/specs/wallet-checkout.md "Session-scoped agent grants".
  gates: [^agent-authorized, ^checkout-authorized]
  signals: [!agent-grant-issued, !agent-grant-revoked, !agent-purchase-initiated]

verify:
  symbol: "#verify-route"
  description: >
    Human-facing ^personhood-verified checkpoint for agent-initiated purchases, opened
    from a link the agent surfaced. Two modes (#worldid-service): in MOCK, GET
    /verify/:sessionId renders a click-through page that settles the order (disabled in
    world mode so it can't bypass the selfie). In WORLD, the SPA renders the World ID
    Selfie Check widget, reads GET /verify-context/:sessionId for the RP signature, and
    POSTs the proof to POST /verify/:sessionId, which verifies it with World before
    settling. Settlement is shared and single-use (via #verification-service +
    #payment-service) either way, in settleVerifiedSession() — which, for
    agent-initiated orders only (order.agent_grant_id set), first re-checks
    ^grant-scope-valid via #agent-grants-service.evaluateGrantScope() (destination,
    per-tx/rolling caps, expiry, revocation) since the grant can be revoked in the
    window between /agent/checkout and the human completing the selfie; a denial marks
    the order failed and returns "grant_denied" (403 grant_scope_denied) without
    charging. Human orders have no agent_grant_id and skip this entirely. It then checks
    ^ledger-confirmed ALONGSIDE ^grant-scope-valid (not instead of it) — only for orders
    whose user has an enrolled Ledger device (#ledger-confirm-service): a denied/timed-out
    approval marks the order failed and returns "ledger_denied" (403); a still-pending
    approval returns "pending_ledger_confirmation" (202) and leaves the order 'pending'
    so the same verification link can be revisited once the human approves on their
    device, without re-running personhood. It then wraps
    executePayment()/markOrderPaid() in a try/catch the same way #checkout-route does:
    any exception marks the order failed via #orders-service.markOrderFailed() and both
    routes return a "payment_failed" outcome (502) instead of leaving the order stuck
    'pending'.
  gates: [^personhood-verified, ^grant-scope-valid, ^ledger-confirmed]
  signals: [!personhood-verified, !purchase-completed]

ledger:
  symbol: "#ledger-route"
  description: >
    Enrolls a human's Ledger device and lets it answer pending ^ledger-confirmed
    approval requests raised by #checkout-route/#verify-route
    (#ledger-confirm-service). GET /ledger/enroll-nonce issues a one-time nonce; POST
    /ledger/enroll binds a signerAddress to the caller once they sign it. GET
    /ledger/pending-approvals returns the caller's own pending, unexpired approvals
    as typed-data payloads to sign. POST /ledger/approvals/:approvalId submits a
    device signature, verified against the STORED order snapshot (never caller-echoed
    fields) — 404 on an approval owned by someone else, so ownership can't be probed.
    GET /ledger/mock/:approvalId is a LEDGER_CONFIRM_MODE=mock-only click-through
    auto-approve for demoing without hardware (409 outside mock mode). All routes
    except the mock one require the caller's own ^authenticated user token (never an
    agent grant) — a human enrolls and answers approvals only for themselves.
  gates: [^authenticated, ^ledger-confirmed]
  signals: [!ledger-device-enrolled, !ledger-approval-verified]

circle-webhook:
  symbol: "#circle-webhook-route"
  description: >
    Receives Circle transaction-state webhooks. Source of truth for terminal
    payment state (COMPLETE/FAILED/DENIED/CANCELLED); updates orders idempotently
    by Circle transaction id. Full design in .paradigm/specs/wallet-checkout.md.
  gates: [^webhook-signature-verified]
  signals: [!payment-confirmed, !payment-failed]
  implementation_plan:
    webhook_signature_verified: >
      Verify X-Circle-Signature (base64 ECDSA over the RAW body) against the public key
      for X-Circle-Key-Id (GET Circle's /v2/notifications/publicKey/{keyId}, cache
      in-process). 401 on missing headers, unknown key id, or bad signature. Requires raw
      body bytes — server/src/index.ts's global express.json() currently discards these;
      switch to express.json({ verify: (req,_res,buf) => { req.rawBody = buf } }) (or a
      route-scoped express.raw()) so this route can verify before parsing.
    order_update: >
      Map Circle's transactionId + state to an `orders` row via
      `update orders set status = $1, updated_at = now() where circle_transaction_id = $2`
      (COMPLETE -> confirmed, FAILED/DENIED/CANCELLED -> matching lowercase status).
      Idempotent by construction — replays set the same terminal value. Emit
      !payment-confirmed / !payment-failed after the row update.
